{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdfYYErypLbfoBqfzymeR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Genre Balance of the CLDW (80 texts)\n\n| Genre | Texts | % of Corpus |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Travelogue | 27 | 33.8% |\n| Guide | 18 | 22.5% |\n| Poetry | 8 | 10.0% |\n| Essay | 7 | 8.8% |\n| Journal | 6 | 7.5% |\n| Epistle | 6 | 7.5% |\n| Survey | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Drama | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Epistle and Journal | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Fiction | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Novel | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Painting | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Miscellany | 1 | 1.3% |\n| Prose Fiction | 1 | 1.3% |\n| **Total** | **80** | **100%** |\n\n## Comment\n\nThe corpus is **markedly uneven**. Two genres — **Travelogue (33.8%)** and **Guide (22.5%)** — together account for **56%** of the corpus (45 of 80 texts). Adding the next four genres (Poetry, Essay, Journal, Epistle) covers all but 8 texts. The remaining **eight genres are singletons** (one text each), forming a long tail of one-offs such as Drama, Novel, Painting, and Miscellany.\n\nThis distribution is unsurprising given the subject: writing *about* the Lake District in this period was dominated by tourism-driven prose (tours and guidebooks), so the imbalance reflects the actual literary output rather than a curation flaw. Still, by any statistical measure the genre distribution is highly skewed — a handful of dominant categories plus a scatter of rare ones — rather than balanced.\n\n(Note: a few genre labels are borderline — e.g. \"Fiction,\" \"Novel,\" and \"Prose Fiction\" are treated here as the corpus's own distinct labels but arguably overlap; collapsing them would still leave the overall picture heavily weighted toward travel writing.)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":54537,"cache_read_input_tokens":2286,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":54537},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"sta